s uniquely defined, LGn enjoys a\nL165@P3: tree structure. The leftmost rotation distance d(T,T \u0003\nL166@P3: ) between T and T \u0003 ∈ Bn is\nL167@P3: the length of the unique path between T and T \u0003 in the directed graph LGn. LGn is\nL168@P3: a subgraph of the graph Gn according to the usual rotation. Algebraic properties\nL169@P3: of Gn can be found in [1, 3, 16, 20, 25, 26].\nL170@P3: 3 Tree structure Bn\nL171@P3: Given T ∈ Bn with T \u0007= 1n and wT , we obtain the weight sequence of the unique\nL172@P3: T \u0003 such that T ❀ T \u0003 in the following way. Let i ≥ 2 be the smallest integer such\nL173@P3: that wT (i) = 1. Let j = max{m ∈ [i, n]|i = m − wT (m)+1}, i.e. the greatest\nL174@P3-4: integer m such that the largest subtree with last leaf m has i as the first leaf. ThenRotational tree structures on binary trees and triangulations 803\nL175@P4: Figure 3: The flipping tree structure T6\nL176@P4: wT\u0002 = wT except for the integer j: wT\u0002 (j)= j. It is worth noting that this integer\nL177@P4: j cannot be modified further since we have 1 ≤ wT (k) ≤ k for all T ∈ Bn and\nL178@P4: k ∈ [1,n].\nL179@P4: The poset (Bn, ∗\nL180@P4: ❀) enjoys some properties which can be easily obtained. (Bn, ∗❀\nL181@P4: ) is a poset with greatest element 1n for which w1n = (1, 2, 3,...,n). This poset has\nL182@P4: a tree structure (with the greatest element 1n as root) and thus is a join-semilattice\nL183@P4: (see Fig. 4 and 5). The poset (Bn, ∗\nL184@P4: ❀) is graded, i.e. there exists an integer-valued\nL185@P4: function r defined on Bn by r(T )= card{i ∈ [1,n]|wT (i)= i} such that T ∗❀ T \u0003\nL186@P4: and r(T \u0003)= 1+ r(T ) iff T ❀ T \u0003. r(T ) is equal to the number of internal nodes\nL187@P4: that are on the left arm of T . We have r(1n)= n − 1.\nL188@P4: Let us remark that Bn is isomorphic to two subtrees of Bn+1. One is obtained\nL189@P4: by sustituting \u0003✷✷ for the last leaf ✷ in all the Bn trees. If (w1,...,wn) ∈ Bn,\nL190@P4: then (w1,...,wn